#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Executive summary] <executive-summary>
This project asks a single decision-relevant question: #emph[given the history of US consumer prices, what inflation rate should a Tunisian bank, treasurer or importer budget for over the next one to five years, and how much confidence can be placed in that number?] The answer is built from the full Box--Jenkins methodology on the monthly US Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (CPI-U, seasonally adjusted), taken from a public Kaggle dataset covering January 1913 to December 2020 and estimated on the post-1957 sample ($n = 768$).

The empirical sequence is the one taught in the course. The price index is non-stationary: its autocorrelation function decays almost linearly and its variance grows with its level, and the augmented Dickey--Fuller and KPSS tests agree that one regular difference is required ($d = 1$). The Box--Cox profile likelihood points to a power transform between the log and the square root; the logarithm is retained because it converts the index into a growth rate and because every conclusion is unchanged under either transform. The differenced series, monthly inflation $pi_t = 100 Delta ln P_t$, is stationary, but its autocorrelation function shows a clear annual echo at lags 11--13, which justifies a #emph[multiplicative seasonal] model rather than a purely non-seasonal one.

Model selection over a grid of multiplicative SARIMA specifications ($p \, q in { 0 \, dots.h \, 3 }$, $\( P \, Q \) in { \( 0 \, 0 \) \, \( 1 \, 0 \) \, \( 0 \, 1 \) \, \( 1 \, 1 \) }$, $d = 1$, $D = 0$) retains, on the joint criterion of lowest BIC among specifications whose residuals pass the portmanteau tests,

$ \( 1 - phi.alt_1 B \) \( 1 - Phi_1 B^12 \) thin Delta y_t #h(0em) = #h(0em) c #h(0em) + #h(0em) \( 1 + theta_1 B + theta_2 B^2 + theta_3 B^3 \) \( 1 + Theta_1 B^12 \) thin epsilon_t \, $<eq:final> i.e.~ARIMA$\( 1 \, 1 \, 3 \) \( 1 \, 0 \, 1 \)_12$ with $y_t = 100 ln P_t$. All eight parameters except the constant are significant at the 1% level, the model is stationary and invertible, and $hat(sigma)_epsilon = 0.259$ percentage points of monthly log inflation. Of the 63 specifications estimated, 54 fail the Ljung--Box test at 12 lags; the selected one does not (12 lags: $p = 0.21$\; 24 lags: $p = 0.10$).

Out of sample, the model is re-estimated on 1957--2015 and used to forecast the 60 months 2016--2020. It attains an RMSE of $1.79$ on the log index against $6.23$ for a random walk, $4.49$ for a random walk with drift and $1.86$ for a seasonal random walk, a $71 %$ reduction in RMSE relative to the naive benchmark. On the index itself the MAPE is $1.59 %$ over five years. The 95% interval covers the realised path throughout, and the one-step-ahead rolling evaluation (RMSE $0.225$) is close to the theoretical innovation standard deviation ($sigma = 0.261$), which is the signature of a correctly specified conditional mean.

In decision terms: the model puts long-run US inflation at about $2.9 %$ a year, implies a half-life of inflation shocks of roughly 16 months, and would have predicted 2020 inflation at $1.7 %$ against a realisation of $1.35 %$. The two episodes it cannot capture are the oil-embargo spike of 1973 and the deflationary collapse of late 2008; both show up as large residuals and both are the source of the volatility clustering that a pure ARIMA model leaves unexplained.

#strong[Keywords:] Box--Jenkins methodology; SARIMA; CPI-U; inflation forecasting; stationarity; Ljung--Box test; forecast evaluation.

= Introduction <introduction>
== The forecasting question <the-forecasting-question>
A price index is one of the few economic series whose future value every business has to budget for. A Tunisian importer quoting in US dollars, a bank pricing a dollar-denominated loan, or a treasury desk setting a nominal return all need a number for US inflation over the life of the contract, together with an honest statement of the uncertainty around it. The forecasting question of this project is therefore:

#quote(block: true)[
#emph[What rate of US consumer price inflation should be expected over horizons of one month to five years, given the history of the CPI up to December 2020, and how wide is the interval within which the realised rate should fall?]
]

The horizon matters. A one-month-ahead forecast is useful for indexation clauses; a five-year-ahead forecast is what a corporate bond or a long-term supply contract requires. The forecast interval is as important as the point forecast, because a financing decision depends on the whole distribution, not on its mean.

== Why a univariate time-series model <why-a-univariate-time-series-model>
The course covers univariate Box--Jenkins modelling, and the question is well matched to it for three reasons. First, inflation persistence is a genuine time-series phenomenon: this month's inflation tells us a great deal about next month's, and a correctly specified ARMA structure exploits that dependence. Second, parsimony has practical value: a model with eight parameters can be re-estimated monthly by a junior analyst, which a large macro-econometric system cannot. Third, and most importantly for a course in forecasting, the framework produces a #emph[quantified] forecast error variance, so the interval around the forecast is derived rather than asserted.

The obvious limitation, stated at the outset, is that a univariate model has nothing to say about the #emph[causes] of inflation --- money growth, import prices, output gaps. It extrapolates the process that generated the past, and it will fail at genuine regime changes. @sec:limits returns to this point.

== Structure of the report <structure-of-the-report>
@sec:data documents the dataset, the sampling decisions and the descriptive statistics. @sec:components treats the components of the series and the transformations of Chapter~2, including the Box--Cox analysis, the ACF/PACF identification and the unit-root tests that fix $d$. @sec:id presents the identification and estimation of Chapter~6 and the three estimation methods of Chapter~4. @sec:diag reports the diagnostic checks. @sec:fcst develops the MMSE forecast recursion of Chapter~5 and evaluates the forecasts against a hold-out sample and three benchmarks. @sec:interpretation translates the statistics into business language, @sec:limits lists the limitations, and @sec:conclusion concludes. The code that produces every number and figure in this report is in the repository described in Appendix~@app:code.

= Data: source, frequency, sample and descriptive statistics <sec:data>
== Source and frequency <source-and-frequency>
The data are the Kaggle dataset #emph["US Inflation Data (updated till May 2021)"] #footnote[#link("https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/varpit94/us-inflation-data-updated-till-may-2021").], distributed as a single CSV file with two columns, `Yearmon` (a date in `dd-mm-yyyy` form, always the first of the month) and `CPI` (the index value). It contains the monthly US Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers, series CPIAUCSL on the Federal Reserve's FRED database, i.e.~the seasonally adjusted index for all items with base 1982--84 $= 100$.

The file holds 1,296 monthly observations from 1913-01 to 2020-12, with no missing month, no duplicated month and no missing value; the acquisition script verifies the SHA-256 digest of the file against the archived copy (`d54f79b8…3ece33c`) so the analysis is reproducible from the raw CSV. @tab:dataaudit summarises the audit.

