== Descriptive statistics <descriptive-statistics>
@tab:descriptives reports the moments of the four working series. Note the contrast that drives everything that follows: the level and the log level have a standard deviation nearly equal to their own mean (76.6 against 125.7, and 76.3 against 458.5), because both are trending, while monthly inflation has a mean of $0.293 %$ and a standard deviation of $0.352 %$ --- a coefficient of variation above unity but no trend at all. Monthly inflation is very mildly right-skewed (0.03) and strongly leptokurtic (excess kurtosis 3.08), so large monthly moves are far more common than a Gaussian model would suggest. Annual inflation, by contrast, is strongly right-skewed (1.56), because the 1970s produced double-digit rates while the floor is bounded below near zero.

