@fig:decades makes the regime structure explicit. Annual inflation averaged $2.3 %$ in the 1960s, $6.8 %$ in the 1970s and $5.4 %$ in the 1980s, fell to $2.95 %$ in the 1990s and $2.5 %$ in the 2000s, and reached $1.75 %$ in the 2010s (@fig:decades). This is not a stationary mean: it is a sequence of regimes, and the largest of them --- the Volcker disinflation, which took annual inflation from a peak of $13.8 %$ in March 1980 to $3.8 %$ by the end of 1982, and below $3 %$ a year later --- is the single most influential event in the sample. @sec:diag shows that the residuals of the final model are dominated by exactly this transition.

