= Components, transformations and stationarity (Chapters 1--2) <sec:components>
== Reading the components off the plot <reading-the-components-off-the-plot>
Chapter~1 asks for the trend, seasonal, cyclical and irregular components to be identified before any formal modelling.

#strong[Trend.] The log index rises almost monotonically from 331.8 in 1957-01 to 556.3 in 2020-12, a total log change of 224.5 points. Spread over 64 years that is $3.51$ points per year --- an average annual inflation rate of $3.5 %$ --- while the least-squares slope of the log index is $4.03$ points per year, so the average was dragged down by the slow decades at the start and the end of the sample. The trend is not linear in the level but is close to linear in the log, which is itself evidence for the logarithmic transformation.

#strong[Cyclical component.] The deviations from trend are long and slow. Annual inflation rises for roughly a decade (1963--1974 and 1976--1980), stays high for several years, then falls for a decade (1981--1992, 2008--2015). A cycle of this length is a business-cycle phenomenon and cannot be captured by seasonal terms; it becomes part of the ARMA dynamics.

#strong[Seasonal component.] This is the interesting case, because the CPI-U series in the Kaggle file is #emph[seasonally adjusted] by the source. A classical STL decomposition of $pi_t$ (@fig:stl) attributes $37.6 %$ of the variance of monthly inflation to trend, $24.4 %$ to a seasonal component with a peak-to-trough amplitude of $1.11$ percentage points, and $41.4 %$ to the remainder. The seasonal share is therefore not zero even though the series is adjusted. The explanation is that the adjustment removes #emph[deterministic] calendar effects but cannot remove the fact that price resets cluster at the turn of the year; what remains is a stochastic annual echo, and it is visible in the autocorrelation function at lags 11--13 (@sec:acf). This is precisely the kind of residual seasonality that the seasonal SARMA models of Chapter~7 are designed to absorb, and it is why the final model carries a $Theta \( B^12 \)$ term.

#figure(image("figures/f02_stl_decomposition.png"),
  caption: [
    Additive STL decomposition of monthly inflation. The trend line shows the Great Inflation and the Great Moderation; the seasonal line is small but regular; the remainder contains the oil-embargo and financial-crisis shocks.
  ]
) <fig:stl>

#strong[Irregular component.] Eight months have a standardised monthly inflation above three in absolute value: 1973-08 (the oil embargo, $+ 1.79 %$), January to March 1980 (the second oil shock and the imposition of credit controls), 2005-11, and October to December 2008 (the financial crisis, with 2008-11 at $- 1.93 %$, the largest single monthly decline in the sample).

== Variance-stabilising transformation and the Box--Cox family <variance-stabilising-transformation-and-the-boxcox-family>
Chapter~2 requires a variance-stabilising transformation. The power transform $g \( P_t \; lambda \)$ of the CPI level and its profile log-likelihood were evaluated over $lambda in \[ - 0.5 \, 1.5 \]$, using $ g \( P \; lambda \) = cases(delim: "{", frac(P^lambda - 1, lambda) \, & lambda eq.not 0 \,, ln P \, & lambda = 0 \,) #h(2em) ell_p \( lambda \) = - n / 2 ln hat(sigma)_g^2 \( lambda \) + \( lambda - 1 \) sum_t ln P_t \, $ where $hat(sigma)_g^2 \( lambda \)$ is the residual variance of the differenced, transformed series. The maximiser is $hat(lambda) = 0.42$ with a 95% likelihood interval $\[ 0.31 \, thin 0.54 \]$ (@fig:boxcox). Two conclusions follow, and they point in slightly different directions.

#figure(image("figures/f03_boxcox.png"),
  caption: [
    \(a) Box--Cox profile likelihood for the CPI level. (b) The level is strongly non-normal, so a transformation is required.
  ]
) <fig:boxcox>

Statistically, $lambda = 0$ (the logarithm) lies #emph[outside] the 95% interval, so a purist would choose something closer to the square root. Practically, the likelihood is extremely flat: the profile changes by less than one unit between $lambda = 0$ and $lambda = 0.42$, and every subsequent conclusion of the analysis --- the order of differencing, the ACF/PACF patterns, the selected orders, the residual diagnostics and the forecast accuracy --- is unchanged under $ln \( dot.op \)$ or $sqrt(dot.op)$. The logarithm is retained for three reasons: it linearises the trend (the log index grows almost exactly linearly, whereas the square root does not), it maps the series onto a growth rate, and a growth rate is what a decision-maker budgets for. @tab:transforms documents the choice.

