Formal confirmation uses the two tests of Chapter~2 in parallel, because their nulls are opposite and the agreement between them is more informative than either test alone. The augmented Dickey--Fuller statistic for $y_t$ is $- 1.29$ ($p = 0.64$) with a constant and $- 1.07$ ($p = 0.93$) with a constant and trend, so the unit-root null cannot be rejected. The KPSS statistic is $4.26$ against a 5% critical value of $0.46$, so stationarity is decisively rejected. Both tests agree that $y_t$ is $I \( 1 \)$.

After one difference, $Delta y_t = pi_t$, the ACF drops to $0.555$ at lag 1, $0.383$ at lag 2 and $0.264$ at lag 3, and the ADF statistic ranges from $- 8.46$ ($p < 0.001$) with three augmentation lags to $- 2.91$ ($p = 0.044$) with twelve, so the unit root is rejected at conventional lag lengths, while the KPSS test on the same series is borderline. The interpretation of that borderline result is important and is taken up in @sec:diag: the full-sample KPSS statistic on $pi_t$ is contaminated by the 1968--1982 regime shift in the #emph[mean] of inflation, not by a stochastic trend. Two facts support this reading. First, over 1980--2020 --- the post-Volcker sample --- the ADF statistic on $pi_t$ is $- 4.94$ ($p < 0.001$), against $- 0.62$ ($p = 0.87$) over 1957--1979. Second, the residuals of the finally selected model pass the Ljung--Box test at every lag up to 24, which could not happen if $pi_t$ retained a unit root.

