  ) <tab:unittest>
The order of differencing is therefore $d = 1$. For the seasonal order, the ACF of $pi_t$ shows significant spikes at lags 11, 12 and 13 ($0.42$, $0.45$ and $0.34$, all far outside the $plus.minus 0.072$ Bartlett band), and the Ljung--Box statistic at lag 12 is $1125$ on 12 degrees of freedom. That is a strong annual echo. It is #emph[not], however, evidence that the series needs seasonal differencing: the ADF test on the annually differenced series gives $- 3.01$ ($p = 0.034$), no better than the monthly difference, and seasonal differencing would over-difference a series whose annual pattern is already partly removed by the source's seasonal adjustment. The modelling decision is accordingly $D = 0$ with a seasonal ARMA block to absorb the echo --- the specification choice examined in @sec:id.

#figure(image("figures/f04_acf_pacf.png"),
  caption: [
    ACF and PACF used for identification. The level (top) has an ACF that decays almost linearly, the signature of $d = 1$. The monthly difference (middle) has an ACF that dies out within a few lags but retains a peak at lag 12, which is the seasonal signature. The annual difference (bottom) is shown as a check.
  ]
) <fig:acf>

Finally, @fig:varstab reports the rolling standard deviation of the level, of the log level and of the monthly difference over 60-month windows. The level's variance more than triples across the sample; the log's variance is far more stable; the differenced series has an essentially constant variance apart from the 1973--1982 episode. This is the graphical counterpart of the Box--Cox result and of the stationarity tests.

#figure(image("figures/f05_variance_stability.png"),
  caption: [
    Rolling 60-month standard deviation. A variance-stabilising transform is required for the level; the monthly difference is heteroskedastic only around the two oil shocks.
  ]
) <fig:varstab>

= Identification and estimation (Chapters 3, 4, 6 and 7) <sec:id>
== The multiplicative seasonal model <the-multiplicative-seasonal-model>
Chapter~7 generalises the ARIMA model to the multiplicative seasonal form $ underbrace(phi.alt \( B \) Phi \( B^s \), upright("AR")) #h(0em) underbrace(\( 1 - B \)^d \( 1 - B^s \)^D, upright("differencing")) #h(0em) y_t #h(0em) = #h(0em) c #h(0em) + #h(0em) underbrace(theta \( B \) Theta \( B^s \), upright("MA")) #h(0em) epsilon_t \, #h(2em) epsilon_t tilde.op W N \( 0 \, sigma^2 \) \, $<eq:sarima> with $B$ the backshift operator, $s = 12$ for monthly data, and $phi.alt \( B \) = 1 - phi.alt_1 B - dots.h - phi.alt_p B^p$, $theta \( B \) = 1 + theta_1 B + dots.h + theta_q B^q$, and the seasonal polynomials defined analogously in $B^12$. With $d = 1$ and $D = 0$ established in @sec:acf, the identifying task is to choose $p \, q$ and the seasonal orders $P \, Q$.

== Identification from the ACF and PACF <identification-from-the-acf-and-pacf>
Chapter~6 prescribes reading the orders from the ACF/PACF of the stationary series. For $pi_t$ the ACF decays geometrically ($0.555$, $0.383$, $0.264$, $0.15$, $0.12$, $0.08$, …) with a superimposed peak at lag 12, and the PACF has a large first spike ($0.555$) and a smaller one at lag 12 ($0.161$). A geometric ACF with a single dominant PACF spike is the signature of an AR(1) or, when the first MA coefficient is also substantial, an ARMA(1,1); a single spike at the seasonal lag in the PACF suggests $P = 1$. The tentative model is therefore ARIMA$\( 1 \, 1 \, 1 \) \( 1 \, 0 \, 1 \)_12$, with low-order alternatives ARIMA$\( 1 \, 1 \, 1 \)$, ARIMA$\( 2 \, 1 \, 1 \)$ and ARIMA$\( 1 \, 1 \, 2 \)$ to be checked.

Identification from the correlogram is a starting point, not an answer. The theoretical ACF of a model with a seasonal MA term has an infinite tail, and sample autocorrelations of a series with 767 observations are estimated with substantial noise. The formal choice is therefore made over a grid, with the information criteria as the criterion and the residual diagnostics as a filter --- which is the procedure Chapter~6 actually recommends.

== Candidate grid and selection <candidate-grid-and-selection>
Every specification in the grid $ \( p \, q \) in { 0 \, 1 \, 2 \, 3 }^2 \, #h(2em) \( P \, Q \) in { \( 0 \, 0 \) \, \( 1 \, 0 \) \, \( 0 \, 1 \) \, \( 1 \, 1 \) } \, #h(2em) d = 1 \, med D = 0 \, $ was estimated by Gaussian maximum likelihood using the Kalman filter, subject to $k lt.eq 10$ estimated parameters, and each was screened with the Ljung--Box test at 12 and 24 lags. @tab:selection reports the best of each family; @fig:selection ranks the fourteen best overall.

