Three results stand out, and each is a lesson rather than a technicality.

First, a purely #emph[non-seasonal] model is not adequate. The best of that family, ARIMA$\( 1 \, 1 \, 3 \)$ with $d = 1$ and no seasonal block, has a BIC of $264.7$ --- a full $100$ points worse than the selected model --- and its residual autocorrelation at lag 12 is $+ 0.213$, far outside the $plus.minus 0.071$ band. Its Ljung--Box $p$-value at 12 lags is below $0.001$: the annual echo survives, and the residuals are not white noise. This is exactly the failure that Chapter~7 warns about when seasonality is ignored.

Second, adding seasonal differencing is the wrong remedy. When $D = 1$ is imposed on the same non-seasonal orders, ARIMA$\( 1 \, 1 \, 1 \) \( 1 \, 1 \, 1 \)_12$ has a BIC of $201.6$ --- still $38$ points worse than the selected model --- and its residuals fail the portmanteau test badly: the Box--Pierce statistic at 24 lags is $60.0$ on 24 degrees of freedom ($p = 0.0001$), even though its #emph[contemporaneous] residual autocorrelation at lag 12 happens to be near zero ($+ 0.001$). Seasonal differencing applied to a series without a seasonal unit root #emph[creates] a seasonal MA structure with a coefficient near $- 1$, the classic over-differencing signature. @sec:acf already reached the same conclusion from the unit-root tests, so $D = 0$ is retained. @fig:seasonalblock shows the like-for-like comparison.

Third, the seasonal block must contain #emph[both] a seasonal AR and a seasonal MA term. The selected model, ARIMA$\( 1 \, 1 \, 3 \) \( 1 \, 0 \, 1 \)_12$, has AICc $= 126.9$ and BIC $= 163.9$. Dropping the seasonal MA term, giving ARIMA$\( 1 \, 1 \, 3 \) \( 0 \, 0 \, 1 \)_12$, raises BIC to $241.2$ and its 24-lag Box--Pierce $p$-value falls to $0.011$\; dropping the seasonal AR term, giving ARIMA$\( 1 \, 1 \, 3 \) \( 1 \, 0 \, 0 \)_12$, raises BIC to $233.9$. Figure ~@fig:seasonalblock isolates the mechanism for the extreme case: with the whole seasonal block removed, the residual autocorrelation at lag 12 is $+ 0.213$, against $- 0.018$ for the selected model.

The selection is therefore not \"lowest BIC\" in a mechanical sense. BIC alone would prefer ARIMA$\( 2 \, 1 \, 1 \) \( 1 \, 0 \, 1 \)_12$, whose BIC of $162.3$ is $1.6$ points lower; that model is rejected because its Ljung--Box $p$-value at 12 lags is $0.026$, so its residuals are not white noise at the 5% level. Adding the third MA term and the AR term of the selected model costs $1.6$ BIC points and buys residuals that pass the portmanteau test at 12 and 24 lags, which is the trade Chapter~6 recommends.

