== The three estimation methods of Chapter 4 <the-three-estimation-methods-of-chapter-4>
Chapter~4 requires the method of moments, least squares and maximum likelihood. All three are applied to the same series, $pi_t$ (@tab:estimators), and the comparison is instructive.

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 6,
    align: (left,right,right,right,right,right,),
    table.header([Parameter], [Moments], [Cond. LS], [Yule-Walker], [Hannan-Rissanen], [MLE],),
    table.hline(),
    [AR(1): $phi.alt_1$], [0.5558], [--], [0.5551], [0.5552], [--],
    [AR(1): $sigma_epsilon^2$], [0.0854], [--], [--], [--], [--],
    [MA(1): $theta_1$], [no real root$""^dagger$], [0.4111], [--], [--], [0.4108],
    [MA(1): $sigma_epsilon^2$], [--], [0.0965], [--], [--], [0.0964],
    [ARMA(1,1): $phi.alt_1$], [--], [0.7188], [--], [0.7391], [0.7178],
    [ARMA(1,1): $theta_1$], [--], [$- 0.2438$], [--], [$- 0.2764$], [$- 0.2432$],
  )]
  , caption: [The three estimation methods of Ch. 4 applied to monthly inflation $pi_t$. The moment estimator for AR(1) is $hat(phi.alt)_1 = r_1$\; for MA(1) it solves $theta^2 + theta \/ r_1 + 1 = 0$ and keeps the invertible root.]
  , kind: table
  ) <tab:estimators>
#strong[Method of moments.] For an AR(1), the Yule--Walker equations give $hat(phi.alt)_1 = r_1$, and for a general AR(p) they give $bold(Gamma)_p bold(phi.alt) = bold(gamma)_p$. Applied to $pi_t$: $r_1 = 0.5558$, so $hat(phi.alt)_1 = 0.5558$ (Burg's recursive algorithm gives $0.5552$, and the Yule--Walker solution $0.5551$ --- the three agree to two decimals). The innovation variance follows from $hat(sigma)^2 = hat(gamma)_0 \( 1 - hat(phi.alt)_1^2 \)$.

For an MA(1) the moments give $rho_1 = - theta \/ \( 1 + theta^2 \)$, so $theta$ solves the quadratic $theta^2 + theta \/ r_1 + 1 = 0$. Here $r_1 = 0.5558$, and the discriminant $1 \/ r_1^2 - 4 = - 0.763$ is #emph[negative]: #strong[the method of moments has no real solution.] This is not a numerical accident. A moment estimator based on the first autocorrelation is feasible only for $\| r_1 \| lt.eq 0.5$, and this series has persistence well above that threshold. The failure is a genuine limitation of the classical method, and it is the reason the course moves on to least squares and maximum likelihood. The applied lesson is that moment estimators are convenient for pure AR models and inadequate for MA components with high persistence.

#strong[Least squares.] Conditional least squares minimises $S \( bold(beta) \) = sum hat(epsilon)_t^2$ with $hat(epsilon)_t = x_t - mu - theta hat(epsilon)_(t - 1)$ and $hat(epsilon)_0 = 0$, which is nonlinear in $theta$. A quasi-Newton search gives $hat(theta) = 0.411$ and $hat(sigma)_(upright("CSS"))^2 = 0.0965$ for the MA(1), and $hat(phi.alt) = 0.719$, $hat(theta) = - 0.244$ for the ARMA(1,1). Hannan--Rissanen --- the two-step estimator that regresses $x_t$ on its own lags to obtain residuals and then uses those residuals as regressors, i.e.~least squares on an #emph[estimated] MA term --- gives $hat(phi.alt) = 0.739$ and $hat(theta) = - 0.276$.

#strong[Maximum likelihood.] The exact Gaussian likelihood, evaluated through the Kalman filter, gives $hat(theta) = 0.4108$ and $hat(sigma)^2 = 0.0964$ for the MA(1) --- indistinguishable from conditional least squares --- and $hat(phi.alt) = 0.7178$, $hat(theta) = - 0.2432$ for the ARMA(1,1), within $0.03$ of the Hannan--Rissanen values and $0.002$ of conditional LS. The agreement is the expected outcome for $n = 767$: conditional and exact likelihood differ only in the treatment of the first few observations, so their relative difference is of order $1 \/ n$. The practical recommendation that follows from @tab:estimators is that for samples of this size the three methods are interchangeable, and the choice should be made on convenience; ML is used for the seasonal model because it handles the seasonal polynomials and the differencing in one consistent likelihood.

== The selected model <the-selected-model>
@tab:params reports the ML estimates. The fitted model is $ \( 1 - 0.9588 B \) \( 1 - 0.9747 B^12 \) thin Delta y_t = 0.0003 + \( 1 - 0.5823 B - 0.1058 B^2 - 0.1281 B^3 \) \( 1 - 0.8676 B^12 \) thin hat(epsilon)_t \, $<eq:fitted> with $hat(sigma)_epsilon = 0.2586$, log-likelihood $- 55.36$, AIC $126.71$, AICc $126.90$ and BIC $163.85$.

