Four features of these estimates deserve comment.

#emph[Stationarity and invertibility.] The inverse AR roots have moduli $0.754$ and $0.244$ (the second from $phi.alt_1$ and $Phi_1$ combined), all below one, so the model is stationary; the inverse MA roots have moduli $0.62$ (from $theta_1$) and $0.87$ (from $Theta_1$), all below one, so it is invertible. The seasonal AR coefficient $hat(Phi)_1 = 0.9747$ is close to the unit circle: the annual component is highly persistent, with a seasonal half-life of about $12 ln 0.5 \/ ln 0.975 approx 330$ months, which is the formal statement of what @fig:stl showed visually --- this is a slow-moving annual pattern, not a calendar effect that resets each year.

#emph[Persistence.] The largest inverse AR modulus is $0.754$, so the half-life of a shock to the log price level is $ln 0.5 \/ ln 0.754 = 16.5$ months. A one percentage-point surprise in monthly inflation therefore leaves about half a percentage point in the annual rate after eighteen months. Inflation is persistent but mean-reverting on a horizon that matters for a one- to three-year contract.

#emph[The constant.] The estimated constant is $hat(c) = 0.0003$ with a standard error of $0.0003$ and a $p$-value of $0.39$, so it is not statistically significant. Dropping it is nevertheless a mistake, and @sec:fcst shows why in out-of-sample terms: a constant of exactly zero in The seasonal model above forces the long-run drift of the log price level, and hence long-run inflation, to be exactly zero, which contradicts both the data and economics. Retained, the implied long-run inflation rate is $hat(c) \/ \[ \( 1 - sum hat(phi.alt) \) \( 1 - hat(Phi)_1 \) \] = 0.0003 \/ \( 0.0412 times 0.0253 \)$, i.e.~$0.243 %$ per month or about $2.9 %$ a year --- within a whisker of the Federal Reserve's 2% target plus the historical bias of the CPI.

#emph[The MA(3) term.] Three MA coefficients, not one, are required, and all three are significant at the 1% level. The need for $theta_3$ reflects the short-run dynamics of monthly inflation: price changes are not a first-order process but reverberate for about a quarter, consistent with the staggered contract structure of the US economy.

@fig:fitted shows that the fitted values track the observed log index closely, and that the residuals are centred on zero with a fairly constant spread, apart from the two known episodes.

#figure(image("figures/f08_fitted_residuals.png"),
