== Zero mean <zero-mean>
The residual mean is $0.00018$ percentage points with a standard error of $0.00939$, so $t = 0.02$ and $p = 0.985$: the null of a zero mean is not remotely rejected, and the 95% interval $\[ - 0.018 \, thin 0.019 \]$ is tight relative to $hat(sigma)_epsilon = 0.259$. There is no systematic bias left in the conditional mean.

== Constant variance <constant-variance>
The residuals are split into two halves of 383 observations. The variances are $0.06779$ and $0.06747$, a ratio of $1.005$ with an $F$-test $p$-value of $0.963$, so the unconditional variance is constant across the sample. Regressing the squared residuals on a time trend gives a slope of $2 times 10^(- 5)$ with $t = 0.55$ ($p = 0.58$), confirming that the variance does not drift with time.

This is where the honest qualification belongs. The #emph[unconditional] variance is constant, but the #emph[conditional] variance is not: the ARCH-LM statistic at 12 lags is $49.6$ ($p < 0.001$) and the Ljung--Box test on the squared residuals gives $76.4$ ($p < 0.001$). Volatility clusters --- the early 1970s, the Volcker years and 2008--2009 are visibly more volatile than the mid-1990s --- and a model with homoskedastic innovations cannot represent that. The consequence for the forecast is precise and worth stating carefully: the point forecasts are unaffected, because they depend only on the conditional mean equation, but the #emph[Gaussian confidence intervals are too narrow in turbulent periods and slightly too wide in calm ones]. A GARCH extension (Tsay, 2010, ch.~3) would fix the intervals; it is outside the scope of this course's Box--Jenkins cycle, and the intervals reported in @sec:fcst are correctly labelled as conditional on homoskedasticity.

== Residual autocorrelation <residual-autocorrelation>
This is the decisive check. The residual ACF and PACF (@fig:resacf) mostly stay within the $plus.minus 0.071$ Bartlett band; the largest absolute autocorrelation over the first twelve lags is $0.098$, at lag 11, and the next largest is well inside the band. The Ljung--Box statistics are $3.27$ at 6 lags ($p = 0.77$), $15.66$ at 12 ($p = 0.21$), $28.36$ at 18 ($p = 0.06$) and $33.10$ at 24 ($p = 0.10$), and the Box--Pierce statistic at 24 lags is $32.44$ ($p = 0.12$). None of these rejects white noise.

One honest caveat: at 36 lags the Ljung--Box $p$-value falls to $0.042$. That marginal rejection must be read in context. A portmanteau statistic computed at 36 lags accumulates 36 sample autocorrelations; with $n = 767$ and no individual autocorrelation outside the band, the test is picking up the small accumulated noise rather than a specific structure, and it is being applied at a lag length far beyond the estimated orders (the rule of thumb is to test at $m approx ln n$ to $2 ln n$, i.e.~7 to 13). It is reported rather than hidden, but it does not outweigh the evidence at 12 and 24 lags.

