== Influence of the largest residuals <influence-of-the-largest-residuals>
The six largest standardised residuals are 2008-11 ($z = - 6.13$), 1973-08 ($z = 6.10$), 2008-10 ($z = - 3.85$) and three months of the 1980 credit-control episode. Re-estimating the model with the two largest observations deleted changes the coefficients by at most $0.058$ --- $22 %$ of one innovation standard deviation --- and leaves every parameter's sign and significance unchanged. The estimates are not driven by outliers; the outliers are genuinely unusual events that the model correctly refuses to fit.

== Residual normality <residual-normality>
The Jarque--Bera test rejects normality overwhelmingly ($p = 6.9 times 10^(- 119)$), with skewness $- 0.25$ and excess kurtosis $4.13$\; the normal probability plot (@fig:qq) shows the characteristic S-shape of a fat-tailed distribution. This matters for the forecast intervals: with excess kurtosis, the $t$-statistic $plus.minus 1.96$ does not correspond exactly to a 95% probability, and the true coverage of a nominal 95% interval built this way is slightly below 95%. The interval is still reported in the standard way --- this is the Gaussian likelihood's own interval --- but the qualification belongs in the interpretation, and @sec:interpretation reports the realised coverage as a check.

