= Forecasting and out-of-sample evaluation (Chapters 5--6) <sec:fcst>
== The MMSE forecast and its error variance <the-mmse-forecast-and-its-error-variance>
Chapter~5 derives the minimum-mean-square-error forecast by conditional expectation. Write the model in its MA$\( oo \)$ form, $y_t = sum_(j gt.eq 0) psi_j epsilon_(t - j)$, with $psi_0 = 1$. Then the forecast is the conditional expectation, $ hat(y)_(t + h \| t) #h(0em) = #h(0em) bb(E) \[ y_(t + h) divides y_t \, y_(t - 1) \, dots.h \] #h(0em) = #h(0em) sum_(j = h)^oo psi_j thin epsilon_(t + h - j) \, #h(2em) e_(t + h \| t) = sum_(j = 0)^(h - 1) psi_j thin epsilon_(t + h - j) \, $ so it is obtained by setting future innovations to their expectation of zero. The forecast error variance is $ upright(V a r) #h(-1em) (e_(t + h \| t)) #h(0em) = #h(0em) sigma^2 sum_(j = 0)^(h - 1) psi_j^2 \, #h(2em) hat(y)_(t + h \| t) #h(0em) plus.minus #h(0em) z_(alpha \/ 2) thin hat(sigma)_epsilon sqrt(sum_(j = 0)^(h - 1) hat(psi)_j^2) $<eq:interval> the second line of which is the Gaussian confidence interval. The $psi$-weights are the impulse responses of the fitted #emph[integrated] model, which for the model of @tab:params begin $psi_0 = 1$, $psi_1 = 1.359$, $psi_2 = 1.619$, $psi_3 = 1.740$, $psi_4 = 1.856$, and by $h = 12$ have flattened to $psi_11 = 2.550$.

The recursion was implemented independently and checked against the Kalman filter's own interval: the two agree to $6 times 10^(- 8)$ at every horizon (@tab:forecastse), which validates both the $psi$-recursion and the fit. The important economic point is in the second row of that table. Because the ARMA terms are mean-reverting, the model's forecast error at $h = 60$ is $plus.minus 8.62$, whereas a random walk's would be $sigma sqrt(60) = plus.minus 2.00$ in #emph[per-month] units, i.e.~the model's interval is about three times tighter after five years than the naive benchmark's. @fig:fvar shows how the gap opens with the horizon.

