== Hold-out design <hold-out-design>
The evaluation holds out the last five years, 2016-01 to 2020-12 ($h = 60$), and re-estimates the model on 1957-01 to 2015-12 ($n = 708$). This is a genuine out-of-sample test: no information after 2015-12 enters the point forecasts. Three benchmarks are used, each of which a practitioner might use in the absence of a model:

- #strong[Random walk]: $hat(y)_(T + h \| T) = y_T$, the forecast implied by "inflation does not change".

- #strong[Random walk with drift]: $hat(y)_(T + h \| T) = y_T + h hat(mu)$, with $hat(mu)$ the mean log change of the training sample.

- #strong[Seasonal random walk]: $hat(y)_(T + h \| T) = y_(T + h - 12)$, the forecast implied by "the same month next year".

