= Derivations <app:derivations>
#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[A.1 The Yule--Walker equations and the moment estimator for MA(1)] <a.1-the-yulewalker-equations-and-the-moment-estimator-for-ma1>
For a stationary AR(p), multiplying $x_t = phi.alt_1 x_(t - 1) + dots.h + phi.alt_p x_(t - p) + epsilon_t$ by $x_(t - k)$ and taking expectations gives $gamma_k = phi.alt_1 gamma_(k - 1) + dots.h + phi.alt_p gamma_(k - p)$ for $k gt.eq 1$, i.e.~$bold(Gamma)_p bold(phi.alt) = bold(gamma)_p$ with $\( bold(Gamma)_p \)_(i j) = gamma_(\| i - j \|)$. Dividing by $gamma_0$ gives the sample estimator $hat(bold(phi.alt))$ from the sample autocorrelations.

For an MA(1), $x_t = mu + epsilon_t + theta epsilon_(t - 1)$, so $gamma_0 = sigma^2 \( 1 + theta^2 \)$, $gamma_1 = sigma^2 theta$ and $gamma_k = 0$ for $k gt.eq 2$. Hence $rho_1 = theta \/ \( 1 + theta^2 \)$, which inverts to $theta^2 + theta \/ rho_1 + 1 = 0$ with roots $theta = \( - 1 \/ rho_1 plus.minus sqrt(1 \/ rho_1^2 - 4) \) \/ 2$. Real roots require $\| rho_1 \| lt.eq 1 \/ 2$, and the invertible root is the one with $\| theta \| < 1$. For $pi_t$, $rho_1 = 0.5558 > 1 \/ 2$, so the discriminant is negative and the moment estimator does not exist.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[A.2 Forecast error variance from the $psi$-weights] <a.2-forecast-error-variance-from-the-psi-weights>
With $y_t = sum_(j gt.eq 0) psi_j epsilon_(t - j)$ and $epsilon$ white noise, $bb(E) \[ y_(t + h) divides y_t \, dots.h \] = sum_(j gt.eq h) psi_j epsilon_(t + h - j)$, so the forecast error is $e_(t + h \| t) = sum_(j = 0)^(h - 1) psi_j epsilon_(t + h - j)$. Since the $epsilon$ are uncorrelated with variance $sigma^2$, $upright(V a r) \( e_(t + h \| t) \) = sigma^2 sum_(j = 0)^(h - 1) psi_j^2$, which is the interval formula above. For an ARIMA$\( p \, d \, q \)$ the $psi_j$ solve $psi_0 = 1$ and $psi_k = theta_k - sum_(j = 1)^(min \( k \, p + d \)) phi.alt_j^(*) psi_(k - j)$, with $phi.alt^(*)$ the coefficients of $\( 1 - B \)^d phi.alt \( B \)$\; numerically it is safer to read them off the fitted model's impulse responses, as the code does.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[A.3 Long-run mean of an ARIMA$\( p \, 1 \, q \)$ with a constant] <a.3-long-run-mean-of-an-arimap1q-with-a-constant>
Because $Delta y_t$ is stationary with mean $mu_Delta = c \/ phi.alt \( 1 \) Phi \( 1 \)$, we have $bb(E) \[ Delta y_t \] = mu_Delta$ and, by summing, $bb(E) \[ y_(t + h) - y_t \] = h mu_Delta$. The quantity $mu_Delta$ is the long-run #emph[monthly] log inflation rate, so the annual rate is $12 mu_Delta$. For the fitted model, $phi.alt \( 1 \) Phi \( 1 \) = \( 1 - 0.9588 \) \( 1 - 0.9747 \) = 0.0412 times 0.0253 = 0.001042$ and $mu_Delta = 0.0003 \/ 0.001042 = 0.243 %$ per month, i.e.~$2.91 %$ per year.

